  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nAn upper trough over the Midwest and another one over the Pacific\nnorthwest are expected to work in tandem (another word for\n'together') by combining into one trough starting tonight. This\ntrough is expected to bring significant rain to a good portion of\nthe area from now through Friday night. The latest ECMWF and GFS\nensembles are in fair agreement this trough will break up this\nweekend. Fairly zonal flow is expected to occur early next week,\nso there is uncertainty regarding precipitation chances since\nshortwave troughs are challenging to predict in this flow pattern.\nIt is typical to only accurately predict shortwave troughs a few\ndays in advance. \n\nFor precipitation amounts through Friday night, the highest\namounts are expected in the eastern half of the area with a few\nlocations exceeding one inch. There is more uncertainty in the\nwestern half of the County Warning Area, but there is still a\ndecent shot of reaching a quarter of an inch. A few spots in the\nsoutheastern counties accumulated 0.5 inches to 1.34 inches with\nthe storms this evening, so it will be important to watch\npotential flooding there if rain rapidly accumulates with any\nfuture embedded thunderstorms. \n\nA marginal risk of excessive rainfall is expected today and Friday.\nThere is a slight risk for excessive rain today. Will hold off on\nany Flood Watches for now.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nIncreased the winds through noon today since observations were a\nbit stronger than the base forecast.\n\nThere is high confidence in rainfall amounts in the east, with\nlow to moderate confidence in the west through Friday night. \n\nThere is moderate to high confidence in reaching gusts to 30 mph\non Fort Peck Lake in correspondence with the Lake Wind Advisory \nin effect through noon today.\n\nThere is moderate confidence that any thunderstorms will remain\nbelow severe limits from now through Friday night.",
